  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1147 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nMoving into midweek, we are continuing to eye another upper-level\ntrough that traverses across the Great Basin and the central\nRockies by Wednesday. Models continue to depict the better forcing\nacross the border in Colorado, however, isolated to scattered\nshowers cannot be ruled out across northern and northeastern New \nMexico. Confidence in meaningful precipitation is still on the \nlow side, maybe a few hundredths or rain at most, with some light\nsnow accumulations over the higher mountains looking possible. \nIncreasing upper-level winds with this system may also make things\nbreezy, but significant wind impacts are not expected.\n\nCooler air will filter into the region behind this disturbance on\nThursday, keeping temperatures slightly below average. Models are\nstill hinting at a trough crossing the state by late \nFriday/Friday night, but moisture looks to be lacking which would \nultimately preclude any mentionable precipitation chances. \nAlthough, a few light sprinkles can't be ruled out across the east\nwhere moisture profiles are slightly more favorable.",
